# Line endings.
#
# text=auto  -> on COMMIT, normalise text files to LF in the stored history.
# eol=crlf   -> on CHECKOUT, write CRLF into the working tree.
#
# Result: the repository is uniformly LF no matter who commits, while local
# working copies stay CRLF for Windows tooling. Line endings stop being part
# of a file's identity, so a three-line change shows as a three-line diff.
#
# This lives in the repo rather than in core.autocrlf on purpose. core.autocrlf
# is a per-machine setting, so it only helps if every clone is configured the
# same way. This file travels with the code.
* text=auto eol=crlf

# ── Must stay LF everywhere ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# eol=crlf above is unconditional and would apply on Render's Linux builder
# too. Anything Linux EXECUTES has to keep LF: a shebang checked out as
# "#!/bin/bash\r" makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally named
# "bash\r", and the error message never mentions line endings.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
Procfile    text eol=lf
*.pem       text eol=lf

# ── Binary: never diff, never convert ────────────────────────────────────
# Without these, git may guess a file is text and corrupt it by rewriting
# bytes that happen to look like line endings.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
*.rar   binary
*.xlsx  binary

# ── Review noise ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The lockfile is generated. Collapsing it in diffs keeps a real review from
# being buried under thousands of dependency lines. It is still committed and
# still versioned -- this only affects how it is DISPLAYED.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
